---

You need a text editor and a browser. You install nothing; the kit loads from a CDN. When you are ready to own the files, the [installation](/docs/install/installation/) page covers the CLI.

## 1. The head

Create `index.html` and paste this into `<head>`. It is the output of the kit's own `buildHead()`: fonts, the stylesheet, the import map for the kit's bare dependencies (`signal-polyfill` and the Embla packages), the one polyfill, the script, and a theme script that paints `data-ui-theme` before first paint.

{% head mode="cdn" /%}

{% callout type="warning" title="Unreleased" %}
The version in these URLs is the kit installed with this site. 0.5 is not on npm yet, so until it ships these URLs resolve to a 0.4 build that does not understand this markup. Pin an exact version in production; the import map's integrity hashes are per byte, so a floating tag would break them on the next release.
{% /callout %}

## 2. A layout

Add a header and a main region. `data-ui="layout"` turns an element into the band grid for its children: everything inside is capped and centered at the `xl` band unless placed elsewhere.

```html
<body>
  <header data-ui="layout" style="--py: 4; --border-b: 1">
    <div style="--display: flex; --items: center; --justify: space-between">
      <a href="/" data-ui="text-lg" style="--font-weight: strong">Northwind</a>
      <nav style="--display: flex; --gap: 1">
        <a href="#menu" data-ui="button" data-button-variant="ghost">Menu</a>
        <a href="#signup" data-ui="button" data-button-variant="primary">Join</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>
  <main data-ui="layout"></main>
</body>
```

Three things happened in that header. `--py: 4` is four steps on the spacing scale. `--border-b: 1` is a one-pixel border in `--color-border`. And the links became buttons through an identity and a preset, not a class.

## 3. A hero

Inside `<main>`:

```html
<section style="--py: 24; --text-align: center">
  <p data-ui="text-eyebrow">Small-batch roasting since 2019</p>
  <h1 data-ui="text-h4" style="--mt: 2; --text-wrap: balance">Coffee worth slowing down for</h1>
  <p data-ui="text-lg" style="--mt: 4; --mx: auto; --max-w: var(--article-sm); --text: var(--color-muted-foreground)">
    Beans roasted every morning, shipped the same day.
  </p>
</section>
```

`text-eyebrow`, `text-h4`, and `text-lg` are typography roles: each bundles family, size, weight, leading, tracking, and case, and every size is fluid. Write the text in sentence case; the eyebrow's capitals come from the role.

## 4. Cards in a grid

```html
<section id="menu" style="--py: 8">
  <div style="--display: grid; --grid-cols: 1; --grid-cols--md: 3; --gap: 4">
    <article data-ui="card" style="--p: 6; --display: flex-col; --gap: 2">
      <span data-ui="badge" data-badge-variant="muted">Espresso</span>
      <h2 data-ui="text-lg">The classic</h2>
      <p data-ui="text-sm" style="--text: var(--color-muted-foreground)">
        A Honduran single origin with notes of dark chocolate and orange peel.
      </p>
    </article>
    <!-- two more cards -->
  </div>
</section>
```

`--grid-cols--md: 3` is a breakpoint tier: one column until the container is 65 characters wide, three from there. Breakpoints read the nearest inline-size container, not the window, so the same card grid works in a sidebar.

## 5. A theme

Open the page: fonts, colors, and form controls already follow the system scheme, because every color role is a `light-dark()` pair. Pin a scheme with `data-ui-theme="dark"` on `<html>` (the head's script does that from a stored choice), or change the look everywhere with a few tokens:

```html
<style>
  @layer overrides {
    :root {
      --color-primary: light-dark(oklch(0.45 0.15 30), oklch(0.8 0.12 30));
      --radius-multiplier: 0.5;
      --ui-button-font-weight: 600;
    }
  }
</style>
```

A token recolors every primary surface; a hook retunes every button. Neither touches your markup. The [theming](/docs/theming/customization/) page has the whole model.

## Next

Add `<ui-debug data-debug-domains="localhost"></ui-debug>` to the body while you work: it warns in the console about a misspelled utility, a tier without a base, or a preset on the wrong element. Then read the [core concepts](/docs/concepts/core-concepts/), or jump to the [API reference](/api/).
